# NimbleParsec cheatsheet

A quick reference into the `NimbleParsec` module, a library for building
text-based parser combinators.

All examples assume `import NimbleParsec` and that the combinator is compiled
into a parser with `defparsec/3`. A parser returns
`{:ok, results, rest, context, line, byte_offset}` on success, or
`{:error, reason, rest, context, line, byte_offset}` on failure. The leading
`combinator \\ empty()` argument is usually supplied by piping, so it is
omitted from the signatures below.

## Defining and composing parsers
{: .col-2}

### [`defparsec(name, combinator, opts)`](`NimbleParsec.defparsec/3`)

```elixir
defmodule MyParser do
  import NimbleParsec

  defparsec :digits, integer(min: 1)
end

MyParser.digits("123")
#=> {:ok, [123], "", %{}, {1, 0}, 3}
```

Use `defparsecp/3` for a private parser, and `defcombinator/3` /
`defcombinatorp/3` for a reusable combinator with no top-level parser.

### [`concat(left, right)`](`NimbleParsec.concat/2`)

```elixir
integer(4)
|> ignore(string("-"))
|> integer(2)
```

Combinators are plain data, so they compose with `|>`. `concat/2` is the
function form of piping two combinators together.

### [`parsec(name)`](`NimbleParsec.parsec/2`)

```elixir
defcombinatorp :date, integer(4)

defparsec :event,
  parsec(:date)
  |> ignore(string(": "))
  |> integer(2)
```

Invokes an already-compiled combinator, avoiding recompilation and cutting
compile time.

### [`empty()`](`NimbleParsec.empty/0`)

```elixir
empty() |> string("hello")
```

The neutral combinator to start building from.

## Consuming input
{: .col-2}

### [`ascii_char(ranges)`](`NimbleParsec.ascii_char/2`)

```elixir
ascii_char([?0..?9])   # one digit
ascii_char(not: ?\n)   # not a newline
```

One ASCII codepoint. Ranges accept `min..max`, a single codepoint,
`{:not, min..max}`, or `{:not, codepoint}`. Use `utf8_char/2` for UTF-8.

### [`string(binary)`](`NimbleParsec.string/2`)

```elixir
string("T")
```

Matches the exact binary `"T"`, or errors with `expected a string "T"`.

### [`ascii_string(range, count_or_opts)`](`NimbleParsec.ascii_string/3`)

```elixir
ascii_string([?a..?z], 2)
utf8_string([?a..?z], min: 1)

# ascii_string([?a..?z], 2) on "abc"
#=> {:ok, ["ab"], "c", %{}, {1, 0}, 2}
```

Takes an exact length or `min:`/`max:`, and returns a binary instead of a
list of codepoints. Use `utf8_string/3` for UTF-8.

### [`integer(count_or_opts)`](`NimbleParsec.integer/2`)

```elixir
integer(4)       # exactly 4 digits
integer(min: 1)  # one or more digits

# integer(4) on "2010" => [2010]
```

### [`bytes(count)`](`NimbleParsec.bytes/2`)

```elixir
bytes(3)

# bytes(3) on "abc" => ["abc"]
```

Consumes the next `count` raw bytes from the input.

### [`eos()`](`NimbleParsec.eos/1`)

```elixir
letters |> eos()
```

Succeeds only when the input has been fully consumed.

## Combining parsers
{: .col-2}

### [`choice(choices)`](`NimbleParsec.choice/3`)

```elixir
choice([string("foo"), string("bar")])
```

Tries each combinator in order and keeps the first that matches.

### [`optional(optional)`](`NimbleParsec.optional/2`)

```elixir
integer(2) |> optional(string("Z"))
```

Matches the combinator zero or one time.

### [`repeat(to_repeat)`](`NimbleParsec.repeat/3`)

```elixir
repeat(ascii_char([?a..?z]))

# on "abcd" => [?a, ?b, ?c, ?d]
# on "1234" => []  (zero times, no error)
```

Matches the combinator zero or more times.

### [`times(to_repeat, count_or_min_max)`](`NimbleParsec.times/3`)

```elixir
times(ascii_char([?a..?z]), min: 2)
times(ascii_char([?a..?z]), max: 4)
times(ascii_char([?a..?z]), 3)
```

Matches at least, at most, or exactly a number of times.

### [`duplicate(to_duplicate, n)`](`NimbleParsec.duplicate/3`)

```elixir
duplicate(ascii_char([?a..?z]), 3)
```

Repeats the *combinator definition* `n` times at compile time.

### [`eventually(eventually)`](`NimbleParsec.eventually/2`)

```elixir
eventually(integer(2))

# "let's meet at 12?" => [12], rest "?"
```

Discards input until the given combinator matches.

### [`repeat_while(to_repeat, while)`](`NimbleParsec.repeat_while/4`)

```elixir
repeat_while(
  ascii_char([]),
  {__MODULE__, :not_comma, []}
)
```

Repeats while the predicate returns `{:cont, context}`. See also
`quoted_repeat_while/4`.

## Transforming results
{: .col-2}

### [`ignore(to_ignore)`](`NimbleParsec.ignore/2`)

```elixir
ignore(string("T")) |> integer(2)
#   "T12" => [12]
```

Discards the output of the given combinator.

### [`replace(to_replace, value)`](`NimbleParsec.replace/3`)

```elixir
replace(string("T"), "OTHER")
|> integer(2)
#   "T12" => ["OTHER", 12]
```

Replaces the output of the combinator with a single value.

### [`map(to_map, call)`](`NimbleParsec.map/3`)

```elixir
ascii_char([?a..?z])
|> map({Integer, :to_string, []})
#   ?a => "97"
```

Applies a function to each result.

### [`reduce(to_reduce, call)`](`NimbleParsec.reduce/3`)

```elixir
letters
|> reduce({Enum, :join, ["-"]})
#   [?a, ?b, ?c] => "97-98-99"
```

Applies a function to all results at once.

### [`wrap(to_wrap)`](`NimbleParsec.wrap/2`)

```elixir
integer(min: 1)
|> integer(min: 1)
|> wrap()
```

Collects the combinator's results into a single list element.

### [`tag(to_tag, tag)`](`NimbleParsec.tag/3`)

```elixir
tag(integer(min: 1), :integer)
#   "1234" => [integer: [1234]]

unwrap_and_tag(integer(min: 1), :integer)
#   "1234" => [integer: 1234]
```

`unwrap_and_tag/3` is the same but unwraps a single result.

### [`post_traverse(to_post_traverse, call)`](`NimbleParsec.post_traverse/3`)

```elixir
combinator
|> post_traverse({__MODULE__, :fun, []})
```

The callback receives the accumulated results and may transform them, thread
`context`, or return `{:error, reason}` to fail. `pre_traverse/3` runs before
the combinator.

## Lookahead
{: .col-2}

### [`lookahead(to_lookahead)`](`NimbleParsec.lookahead/2`)

```elixir
lookahead(string("if"))
|> ascii_string([], min: 1)
```

Succeeds only if the combinator is ahead, without consuming it.

### [`lookahead_not(to_lookahead)`](`NimbleParsec.lookahead_not/2`)

```elixir
lookahead_not(string("end"))
|> ascii_char([])
```

Succeeds only if the combinator is *not* ahead.

## Errors, position & debugging
{: .col-2}

### [`label(to_label, label)`](`NimbleParsec.label/3`)

```elixir
integer(2)
|> ascii_char([?a..?z])
|> label("a digit then a letter")
```

Replaces the auto-generated `expected ...` error message.

### [`line(to_wrap)`](`NimbleParsec.line/2`)

```elixir
line(integer(min: 1))
#=> {result, {line, offset}}

byte_offset(integer(min: 1))
#=> {result, byte_offset}
```

Attaches the line (or, with `byte_offset/2`, the byte offset) to the result.

### [`debug(to_debug)`](`NimbleParsec.debug/2`)

```elixir
integer(2) |> debug()
```

Prints the combinator state to `:stderr` at parse time.

### [`generate(parsec)`](`NimbleParsec.generate/1`)

```elixir
choice([string("foo"), string("bar")])
|> generate()
#=> "foo" or "bar"
```

Builds a random binary that the parser accepts — handy for tests.
